\begin_layout Abstract
Explainability is hard to achieve in the field of RL, where the great successes
 relies on black box approaches like neural networks.
 In many domains in industry and science, Genetic programming (GP) offers
 the opportunity to automatically generate programs that are interpretable,
 but often represent mathematic expressions which are still hard to fully
 understand completely.
 For a domain expert, it would be very helpful to be shown where his own
 strategy could be improved.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Lösungen mit GP ohne Vorgaben sind oft naja.
 wir versuchen deshalb, GP zu nutzen um Lösungen zu finden, die bekannte
 Lösungsstrategien mit möglichst geringfügigen Änderungen verbessern können.
 Therefore, the concept of familiarity is introduced as quality criterion
 for GP, which measures the deviation from an expert's original programme
 instead of pure complexity.
 This is realised with the tree edit distance as measure, which reflects
 the number of changes to the reference program.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The approach was testet on the mountaincar and the industrial benchmark,
 with several different neuropolicies provdiding transition samples als
 Basis für den Regressionsfehler.
 The results have shown no major drawbacks in terms of regression error
 and fitness, while staying closer to the domain experts approach.
 The real fitness evaluation seems to have a slight tendency for more stable
 results, but seem to depend rather on the volatility of the black box and
 how good the result could be achieved.
\end_layout

