\begin_layout Chapter
Introduction 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Enormous progress in RL has made it possible to outperform human solutions
 even in highly complex domains.
 In RL, an agent without prior knowledge searches for a strategy to maximize
 reward in an environment.
 The greatest advances have only been possible with the help of using highly
 complex structures such as neural networks.
 These are not explainable to humans and cannot domain experts to learn
 from them is very costly.
 Therefore, not only from a scientific point of view, but especially in
 sensitive areas of industry, explainability has become more and more in
 focus, where small wrong decisions can often lead to big damages.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A robust explanation for undesirable developments is especially required
 in front of court.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to be able to profit from the bias-free strategies of a machine,
 there is genetic programming in the field of ML.
 With the help of evolutionary strategies, this can search for formulas
 that can be interpreted by a human.
 In order to gain understanding, they thus offer a domain expert a good
 point of reference, but the solution path often deviates strongly from
 the strategy that an expert would choose.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This thesis therefore introduces the principle of familiarity, by means
 of which solutions are characterised as explainable the less they deviate
 from the expert's solution.
 For this purpose, the complexity measure of classical GP, which is based
 on the number of nodes of the programming tree, is calculated as the number
 of necessary changes to the expert's programming tree.
 This is implemented with the help of the tree edit distance, which is already
 known as a problem in theoretical computer science.
 Thus, genetic programming would still try to achieve the same goal, but
 would be more bound to the heuristic given by the expert.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this work, the goal of GP is generated concretely as a regression problem
 on transition samples of a non-specific black box policy.
\end_layout

