\begin_layout Paragraph
Performance and Explainability Tradeoff
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every learning approach is subject to a trade-off between performance and
 explainability.
 The best explanation is probably the explanation that existed before a
 model, so that the model is a good representation of the explanation.
 How explainable something is is difficult to determine, but humanly written
 approaches can at least be used as a safe anchor point for explainability.
 For the concept of familiarity used in this work, however, they play a
 special role, which is why the following will briefly discuss why they
 are so significant as a reference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Speech
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The ability to speak, or express thoughts in language, is automatically
 learned by every human and plays an important role for human understanding.
 Large parts of the philosophical world even regard speech as the limits
 of what a human can understand.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The limits of my language mean the limits of my world.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, Ludwig Wittgenstein, a mentor of Alan Turing
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wittgenstein1953philosophical"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 Because conscious action is based on language, an action can automatically
 be explicitly formulated in a language and is thus explainable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Secure training process
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

All human exploration is based on monitored by the amygdula for dangers
 and the learned models are thus endowed with a high degree of security.
 Tasks that one does not seem to be able to solve are aborted due to frustration.
 A machine, however, is forced to improve its approach until it has become
 good enough and has to rely on random exploration.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Leaded exploration with early feedback
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

A human learning process is mostly guided and even in experts peer-review
 their ideas before using new strategies, with the sub-goals widely synched
 between interacting humans.
 This largely reduces the risc of over- and underfitting.
 One notable example is that successful models can be implemented in all
 people (such as the Pythagorean theorem) as reliable anchor point.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Let's consider, for example, the game Puzzle as an NP-hard problem, where
 a whole picture has to be assembled from a pile of pieces.
 People are mostly synchronized between essential subgoals like recognizing
 colors and shapes, turning puzzle pieces to the colored side and knowing
 about the picture on the box as reference point.
 A popular tip for beginners is therefore that one should start with the
 edge of the puzzle as pieces can be matched early to a frame.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A RL agent can already fail at all the subgoals very badly.
\end_layout

\end_inset

Teaching a neural network requires an extensive training process and may
 cause unexpected side effects.
 
\end_layout

