\begin_layout Section
Genetic Programming 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "gp_Genetic-Programming"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Searching all components of a formula is an infinitely complex problem and
 therefore normally the task of the spearhead of human abstraction ability,
 the computer scientist.
 In machine learning, however, there are metaheuristic strategies that use
 evolutionary strategies which allow automated creation of computer programs.
 Genetic programming (GP) was introduced by Koza and implements the components
 of programs as genes in a programming tree.
 A population of individuals are optimised over generations through selection
 and mutation of individual genes.
 The selection process requires a fitness function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A new population is largely generated from a selection of the best candidates
 of the last generation.
 This allows successful gene sequences to multiply.
 New candidates are generated by random gene mutations without having to
 search for a completely new solution, and by crossing candidates, the best
 components of two parent trees can combine by randomly crossing two branches
 in their children.
 The rest of the population is replenished with randomly generated candidates
 to guarantee a constant stream of new solutions.
\end_layout

