\begin_layout Paragraph
Evolution Concepts 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The concept of closure refers to the requirement that the creation of trees
 must alays result in evaluable expressions.
 In order to avoid data type complications, Strongly Typed Genetic Programming
 (STGP) is mostly used nowadays, where a conversion between number and truth
 values must be done explicitly.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Operators for explicit conversion are, for example, comparison operators
 or if statements
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Strongly_typed_GP"

\end_inset

 Evaluation safety poses a particular problem because even type-correct
 expressions can be mathematically invalid.
 In the case of frequently occurring functions, the definition gaps are
 usually filled with a dummy value (for example, division by zero with 1).
 To avoid changing the essence of an operator too much, a tree can also
 simply be discarded in the case of rarely occurring errors.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Restricting the search space from complexity bloat 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "gp-complexity-depth-node-based"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to the high branching factor for solution candidates, the genotypic
 search space for trees must be strongly restricted.
 Thus, both the maximum tree depth and the number of nodes are usually limited.
 Formula components can also be additionally weighted to avoid complex mathemati
cal operators, for example the default weighting of the popular GP Framework
 EUREKA can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:EUREKA-GP-complexities"

\end_inset

.
 As a multi-objective optimisation, instead of saving only the best candidate,
 all pareto-efficient combinations of fitness and complexity are stored
 on a pareto front, between which a choice can be made at the end of the
 run.
\end_layout

