\begin_layout Subsection
Symbolic Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The symbolic regression is a common and important use case of GP for this
 approach, which is used to calculate how well the formula can explain data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The regression error is the sum of the distance between the outouts and
 predicted values of a data set.
 This makes it possible to determine fitness by regression on transition
 samples of a neuropolicy.
 With online learning, the reward of the real benchmark is evaluated, offline
 training takes place on a surrogate model instead and therefore has less
 significance.
 Training on surrogate models are nevertheless useful as they can be evaluated
 cost-effectively and in parallel, but especially with industrial controllers,
 the risk of destroy the benchmark itself, is reduced.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both the mean average error (MAE) and the mean square error (MSE) are widely
 used distance measurements for this purpose.
 The MAE is widely used in GP, as it indicates the average deviation from
 the target solution and is therefore distance-true.
 The MSE squares the distance and thus is not distance-true, making it a
 less honest indicator.
 However, in the MSE, outliers and strong deviations are punished more severely,
 which also allowed the black box to be approached more quickly and more
 reliably.
 This also means that volatile solutions are punished more severely.
 
\end_layout

