\begin_layout Section
Familiarity Measurement with the Tree Edit Distance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to generate solution candidates that are as familiar as possible,
 we need to define a distance function on a suitable unit of measurement
  which represents the scope of the change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The mandatory prerequisites for a distance measure are: 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "xaiMeasurementRequirements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$d(x,y)
\backslash
ge0$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (non-negativity)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$d(x,y)=0
\backslash
Leftrightarrow x=y$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (identity of indiscernibles) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$d(x,y)=d(y,x)$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (symmetry) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$d(x,y)
\backslash
le d(x,z)+d(z,y)$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (triangle inequality) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tree edit distance (TED) marks the logical resulting distance measurement
 considering the node-count based paradigm most GP implementations base
 are based on (presented in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "gp-complexity-depth-node-based"

\end_inset

).
 The TED is calculated as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

distance from two labeled ordered trees T and T' by the minimum cost sequence
 of edit operations 
